vivaQuestions: [
{ question: "What is Algorithm Analysis?", answer: "Sir, algorithm analysis is the process of determining the amount of resources, primarily time and memory, that an algorithm requires to execute based on the input size." },
{ question: "What is Time Complexity?", answer: "Sir, time complexity is a theoretical measure that estimates the running time of an algorithm as a function of the length of its input, typically using Big-O notation." },
{ question: "What is Space Complexity?", answer: "Sir, space complexity is the amount of working memory or RAM an algorithm needs during its execution, which includes auxiliary space and the space used by the input." },
{ question: "What are Asymptotic Notations?", answer: "Sir, asymptotic notations are mathematical tools used to describe the running time of an algorithm in the limit, meaning how the time grows as the input size approaches infinity." },
{ question: "What does Big-Oh (O) notation represent?", answer: "Sir, Big-Oh notation represents the upper bound of an algorithm's running time. It describes the worst-case scenario or the maximum time required." },
{ question: "What does Big-Omega (Ω) notation represent?", answer: "Sir, Big-Omega notation represents the lower bound of an algorithm's running time. It gives the best-case scenario or the minimum time required." },
{ question: "What does Big-Theta (Θ) notation represent?", answer: "Sir, Big-Theta notation represents the tight bound. It means the algorithm's running time is bounded both above and below by the same function, indicating the exact growth rate." },
{ question: "What is the Worst-case analysis?", answer: "Sir, worst-case analysis calculates the maximum time taken by an algorithm for any input of size n. It provides a guarantee that the algorithm will never take longer than this time." },
{ question: "What is the Best-case analysis?", answer: "Sir, best-case analysis calculates the minimum time an algorithm will take for a specific, ideal input of size n, such as sorting an already sorted array." },
{ question: "What is Average-case analysis?", answer: "Sir, average-case analysis determines the expected running time over all possible inputs of size n, usually assuming a uniform probability distribution of the inputs." },
{ question: "What is Amortized Analysis?", answer: "Sir, amortized analysis averages the time required to perform a sequence of operations over all the operations. It guarantees the average performance of each operation in the worst case." },
{ question: "What is a Loop Invariant?", answer: "Sir, a loop invariant is a condition or property that is true before the loop starts, true before each iteration, and true after the loop terminates, helping to prove the algorithm's correctness." },
{ question: "What is Bubble Sort?", answer: "Sir, Bubble sort is a simple sorting algorithm that repeatedly steps through the list, compares adjacent elements, and swaps them if they are in the wrong order." },
{ question: "What is the time complexity of Selection Sort?", answer: "Sir, the time complexity of Selection Sort is O(n^2) in all cases—best, worst, and average—because it always scans the entire remaining array to find the minimum element." },
{ question: "How does Insertion Sort work?", answer: "Sir, Insertion sort builds the sorted array one item at a time. It takes an element from the unsorted part and inserts it into its correct position in the sorted part, much like arranging playing cards." },
{ question: "What is Heap Sort?", answer: "Sir, Heap sort is a comparison-based sorting algorithm that uses a binary heap data structure. It builds a max-heap and repeatedly extracts the maximum element to sort the array in O(n log n) time." },
{ question: "What are Linear Time Sorting algorithms?", answer: "Sir, linear time sorting algorithms, like Counting Sort, Radix Sort, and Bucket Sort, do not use comparisons. They use specific properties of the numbers to sort the data in O(n) time." },
{ question: "How does Counting Sort achieve linear time?", answer: "Sir, Counting Sort counts the occurrences of each distinct element and uses arithmetic to calculate their exact positions in the output array, bypassing traditional O(n log n) comparison limits." },
{ question: "What is the difference between Radix Sort and Bucket Sort?", answer: "Sir, Radix sort sorts numbers digit by digit starting from the least significant digit. Bucket sort divides elements into several 'buckets', sorts each bucket individually, and then concatenates them." },
{ question: "Why is analyzing control statements important?", answer: "Sir, analyzing control statements like loops and recursive calls is crucial because they form the core structure that dictates how many times an operation is executed, directly determining the time complexity." }
],
