vivaQuestions: [
{ question: "What is the class P?", answer: "Sir, class P consists of all decision problems that can be solved by a deterministic Turing machine in polynomial time. These are the problems we consider 'easy' or efficient to solve, like sorting or shortest path." },
{ question: "What is the class NP?", answer: "Sir, class NP stands for Non-deterministic Polynomial time. It consists of all decision problems for which a proposed solution can be verified for correctness in polynomial time, even if finding the solution takes exponential time." },
{ question: "Does P equal NP?", answer: "Sir, P vs NP is the biggest unsolved problem in computer science. If P=NP, it means every problem whose solution can be verified quickly can also be solved quickly. Currently, it is widely believed that P does not equal NP." },
{ question: "What is a Polynomial Reduction?", answer: "Sir, it is a method of converting one problem into another in polynomial time. If we can reduce problem A to problem B in polynomial time, it means solving B is at least as hard as solving A." },
{ question: "What makes a problem NP-Hard?", answer: "Sir, a problem is NP-Hard if every single problem in the class NP can be polynomially reduced to it. It means the problem is at least as hard as the hardest problems in NP." },
{ question: "What is the class NP-Complete?", answer: "Sir, a problem is NP-Complete if it satisfies two conditions: first, it must belong to the class NP, and second, it must be NP-Hard. They are the hardest problems inside NP." },
{ question: "Can you name a famous NP-Complete problem?", answer: "Yes sir, the Travelling Salesman Problem (Decision version), the Knapsack problem, Graph Coloring, and the Boolean Satisfiability (SAT) problem are all classic NP-Complete problems." },
{ question: "What is the Travelling Salesman Problem (TSP) in this context?", answer: "Sir, the decision version of TSP asks: 'Given a set of cities and a budget, is there a route that visits all cities and costs less than the budget?' This is heavily proven to be NP-Complete." },
{ question: "What is the Hamiltonian Cycle problem?", answer: "Sir, a Hamiltonian cycle is a closed loop in a graph that visits every single vertex exactly once. Determining if such a cycle exists in an arbitrary graph is NP-Complete." },
{ question: "What are Approximation Algorithms?", answer: "Sir, since we cannot solve NP-Complete problems exactly in polynomial time, we use approximation algorithms. These run in polynomial time and guarantee a solution that is close to the optimal, within a known factor." },
{ question: "What are Randomized Algorithms?", answer: "Sir, randomized algorithms use random numbers in their logic to make decisions, often to achieve better average-case time complexity or to find an approximate solution quickly, like Randomized Quick Sort." },
{ question: "What is Cook's Theorem?", answer: "Sir, Cook's Theorem (or Cook-Levin theorem) was a breakthrough that proved the Boolean Satisfiability problem (SAT) is NP-Complete, establishing the very first NP-Complete problem for others to reduce to." },
{ question: "What does it mean for a problem to be intractable?", answer: "Sir, an intractable problem is one that can be solved in theory, but in practice, it takes so long (like exponential time O(2^n)) that it is impossible to compute for large inputs." },
{ question: "What is the class P-SPACE?", answer: "Sir, P-SPACE is the class of problems that can be solved using a polynomial amount of memory space, regardless of the time taken. It is believed to be much larger than NP." },
{ question: "How do you prove a new problem is NP-Complete?", answer: "Sir, to prove problem X is NP-Complete, we first show X is in NP. Then, we take a known NP-Complete problem (like SAT) and prove it can be polynomially reduced to X." },
{ question: "Are NP-Hard problems always in NP?", answer: "No sir, NP-Hard problems do not have to be in NP. For example, the Halting Problem is NP-Hard but not in NP, because it is undecidable and its solutions cannot even be verified." },
{ question: "Why do we only talk about 'Decision Problems' in P and NP?", answer: "Sir, to establish a formal mathematical framework, computational complexity theory restricts problems to yes/no answers. However, optimization problems can easily be converted into decision problems." },
{ question: "What is an optimization problem?", answer: "Sir, an optimization problem asks for the 'best' solution, like 'What is the shortest route?'. Its decision counterpart asks 'Is there a route shorter than length K?'." },
{ question: "Why are Approximation algorithms useful?", answer: "Sir, for industries dealing with TSP (like logistics), they cannot wait billions of years for a computer to find the perfect route. An approximation algorithm gives a 'good enough' route in seconds." },
{ question: "What is the Vertex Cover problem?", answer: "Sir, it asks if we can find a subset of k vertices such that every edge in the graph touches at least one of these vertices. It is a famous NP-Complete problem." }
],
